"""
LLM-judged relevance ground truth -- an alternative to the taxonomy-formula
ground_truth.json.

Every judgment below was produced by directly reading the hirer's request
text against each pooled candidate provider's profile text (title, about,
services offered, relevant experience) and assessing genuine content fit --
not by checking whether speciality_id/category_id matched. This addresses a
real methodological gap in the original evaluation: ground_truth.json's
relevance score is a deterministic function of the same taxonomy IDs used
to *generate* the text, so retrieval methods were effectively being scored
against a formula, not an independent judgment of whether the match is
actually good.

Pooling methodology (standard TREC-style, to make judging ~2,056 pairs
tractable instead of the full 104x130=13,520): for each hirer, only the
union of the top-10 results from BM25 (refined), Dense (full-dim), and RRF
(sparse=0.7/dense=1.3) are judged (see pipeline/data/judging_pools.json,
built by a one-off script, not part of this file). Anything no method
surfaced is treated as not relevant to any method's ranking anyway, so it
cannot affect Precision/NDCG at the cutoffs actually evaluated.

Grading scale (ordinal, 0-3 -- TREC-style graded relevance):
    3 = excellent match: provider's specific expertise directly addresses
        the hirer's specific need, budget/seniority broadly compatible
    2 = good match: genuinely relevant domain/skillset, but not a perfect
        fit (adjacent sub-focus, or a real but non-ideal seniority/budget
        mismatch)
    1 = weak/tangential match: some genuine surface relevance (adjacent
        category, transferable skill) but not a real fit for this specific
        need
    0 = not relevant: no genuine content-level relevance

Run pipeline/compare_llm_vs_formula.py after this file is complete to
compare against the taxonomy-formula ground truth and re-evaluate all three
retrieval approaches against these judgments.
"""

